Anti-Diabetic-Induced Hypoglycemia
1. Patient Details

Patient Initials / Identifier: E.V.

Age: 75 years

Gender: Female

Weight: 54 kg

2. Suspected Adverse Reaction

Description of Reaction: Severe hypoglycemia episode with blood glucose of 38 mg/dL, causing diaphoresis, confusion, trembling, and loss of consciousness.

Date of Reaction Onset: Week 2 of medication usage following poor oral intake.

Severity: Severe (required emergency glucose administration).

3. Suspected Medication(s)

Primary Suspect: Glibenclamide (Glyburide) 5 mg daily for Type 2 Diabetes.

Date Started: 14 days prior

Date Stopped: Day of ED admission

Action Taken: Glibenclamide discontinued; patient transitioned to a shorter-acting agent.

4. Concomitant Medications

Medication: Atorvastatin 20 mg daily.

5. Outcome & Action Taken

Action Taken: IV Dextrose (D50) administered in ED; long-acting sulfonylurea discontinued.

De-challenge Outcome: Blood glucose levels stabilized; cognitive baseline restored.

Re-challenge: Not re-challenged with long-acting sulfonylureas.

Patient Outcome: Recovered completely.
